const NET_YEARS=[{"y":2020,"guaranteed_share":"not established in the pages opened","asia_share":"not established in the pages opened","note":"The pandemic year: Art Basel/UBS (as relayed by CNN) put 2020 public auction sales at about $17.6bn, down 30%, while online sales doubled to $12.4bn. The two trophies of the year came from outside the New York system — Wu Bin’s ‘Ten Views of a Lingbi Rock’ (RMB 512.9m, Poly Beijing, October, consigned by an American collector who paid $1.21m in 1989, fought over by two bidders for nearly an hour) and Sanyu’s ‘Five Nudes’ (HK$304m, Christie’s Hong Kong, July). In January Sotheby’s guaranteed a Tiepolo ‘without the support of a third party’ and, per e-mails later reported, its owner Patrick Drahi ‘picked up the tab’. Sotheby’s own 2020 total was about $5bn (The Art Newspaper’s prior-year comparison).","src":[["CNN Style, May 2021","https://edition.cnn.com/style/article/basquiat-new-york-auction-week"],["The Value, October 2020","https://en.thevalue.com/articles/poly-auction-ten-views-lingbi-stone-handscroll-ming-dynasty-record"],["The Art Newspaper, 20 January 2023","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2023/01/20/guarantees-withdrawals-and-billionaires-bidding-at-their-own-auction-houses-flagging-market-art"],["The Art Newspaper, 15 December 2021","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2021/12/15/sothebys-73bn-sales-2021-highest-total-company-history"]],"vf":"U"},{"y":2021,"guaranteed_share":"no market-wide share found; the Macklowe sale ($676.1m) was 100% guaranteed by Sotheby’s, with irrevocable bids on 22 of 35 lots (The Art Newspaper)","asia_share":"Sotheby’s: Asian clients placed one-third of bids by value in its worldwide auctions and 46% of bids on works over $5m (The Art Newspaper, December 2021)","note":"The rebound year. Sotheby’s reached $7.3bn, the highest in its history, with 39% of buyers new to the house and online sales above $800m. Divorce and estates fed the top: the court-ordered Macklowe sale ($588.7m hammer), the Solow estate’s unguaranteed Botticelli ($80m hammer, seemingly to a Russian client, with an Asian underbidder), and a living collector’s Basquiat ‘In This Case’ ($81m hammer, three phone bidders). Asian money was visible at the top: Justin Sun took the Macklowe Giacometti through Sotheby’s Hong Kong, and Patti Wong’s phone took the $82.5m Rothko. The crypto bubble produced Beeple’s $69.3m NFT, bought by Singapore-based Vignesh Sundaresan through Metapurse. Other lots at $40m+ found this year but not added: Basquiat ‘Versus Medici’ ($50.8m, Sotheby’s, 12 May) and Twombly ‘Untitled (Rome)’ ($41.6m, Sotheby’s, 12 May).","src":[["The Art Newspaper, 15 December 2021","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2021/12/15/sothebys-73bn-sales-2021-highest-total-company-history"],["ArtfixDaily, 16 November 2021","https://artfixdaily.com/news_feed/2021/11/16/8487-crypto-mogul-snaps-up-78-million-giacometti-records-fall-in-sothe"],["ArtfixDaily, 12 May 2021","https://artfixdaily.com/news_feed/2021/05/12/3707-basquiat-brings-931-million-at-christies-records-toppled-at-sothe"],["The Art Newspaper, January 2021","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/news/botticelli-portrait-sells-for-at-sotheby-s-in-new-york"]],"vf":"S"},{"y":2022,"guaranteed_share":"Pi-eX, November 2022 New York evening sales: 42% of lots ‘effectively pre-sold to external guarantors’ (share of lots); ArtTactic: $1.46bn of post-war and contemporary art carried guarantees in 2022 (Apollo)","asia_share":"not established in the pages opened (Christie’s said only that it had ‘registered bidders across the globe’)","note":"The peak year, and the year of the estate. Christie’s May week made $1.44bn: the Ammann Foundation’s Marilyn ($195m to Larry Gagosian, proceeds to a children’s charity), Anne Bass’s estate ($363.1m, 129% above low estimate, Monet ‘Le Parlement’ $76m after seven bidders) and Maezawa’s Basquiat at Phillips ($85m, bought for $57.3m in 2016). Mercedes-Benz sold its own Uhlenhaut coupé for €135m to a client of Simon Kidston, proceeds to a scholarship fund. In November the Paul G. Allen estate’s 60 lots made $1.296bn hammer ($1.5bn with fees), every lot guaranteed, net proceeds to the Allen Family Foundation; Sotheby’s sold Warhol’s ‘White Disaster’ ($84.5m) for an unnamed consignor. Guarantors named in the trade as the market’s backstops: Peter Brant, Pierre Chen, David Geffen, the Nahmads and the Mugrabis (Apollo). Other $40m+ lots found but not added: Van Gogh ‘Champs près des Alpilles’ ($51.9m), Picasso ‘Tête de femme (Fernande)’ 2022 cast ($48.5m), Rothko ‘No. 1’ Bass ($49.6m), Leutze ‘Washington Crossing the Delaware’ ($45m), Degas ‘Petite danseuse’ Bass ($41.6m), all Christie’s New York May 2022; Botticelli ‘Madonna of the Magnificat’ Allen ($48.4m, 9 November).","src":[["The Art Newspaper, 20 January 2023","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2023/01/20/guarantees-withdrawals-and-billionaires-bidding-at-their-own-auction-houses-flagging-market-art"],["Apollo, ‘Handle with care’","https://apollo-magazine.com/?p=1031310"],["Christie’s, May 2022 marquee week results","https://www.christies.com.cn/en/stories/20-21-may-2022-marquee-week-sale-results-892c301bd67746f4b8a88a05d30404ad"],["The Art Newspaper, 10 November 2022","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2022/11/10/paul-allen-evening-sale-christies-record-1-5-billion"],["Artnet News, ten most expensive works of 2022","https://news.artnet.com/market/ten-most-expensive-works-sold-auction-2022-2234325"],["ArtfixDaily, 19 May 2022","https://artfixdaily.com/news_feed/2022/05/19/4928-rare-mercedes-zooms-to-record-setting-142-million-at-secret-aucti"]],"vf":"S"},{"y":2023,"guaranteed_share":"no market-wide share found; the Emily Fisher Landau evening sale (31 lots, $406.4m) was 100% guaranteed, and 13 lots sold at or below low estimate, ‘very likely to the guarantors’ (Apollo)","asia_share":"not established in the pages opened; Christie’s 2023 total was $6.2bn (its 2024 release)","note":"The year the market turned down while estates kept the top end alive. Sotheby’s sold Emily Fisher Landau’s Picasso ‘Femme à la montre’ ($139.4m) inside a fully guaranteed 31-lot sale, and in June a family that had owned Klimt’s ‘Dame mit Fächer’ since 1994 sold it in London for £85.3m with a third-party irrevocable bid, to a Hong Kong client of Patti Wong after a ten-minute duel with Sotheby’s own Asia desk. Christie’s full-year total fell to $6.2bn. Lots at $40m+ known from memory but not confirmed in this session are listed under ‘unconfirmed’.","src":[["Apollo, ‘Handle with care’","https://apollo-magazine.com/?p=1031310"],["The Art Newspaper, 27 June 2023","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2023/06/27/klimt-portrait-sothebys-most-expensive-work-auction-europe"],["Artnet News, November 2023","https://news.artnet.com/market/emily-fisher-landau-sothebys-results-2391881"],["Artdependence, Christie’s 2024 results","https://artdependence.com/articles/christies-projects-57b-global-sales-in-2024-down-from-last-year-s-62-b"]],"vf":"S"},{"y":2024,"guaranteed_share":"ArtTactic: the value of post-war and contemporary art under guarantee ‘fell to $858m’ in 2024, from $1.46bn in 2022 (Apollo); no lot share found","asia_share":"Christie’s: Asia-Pacific buyers accounted for 26% of auction sales in 2024 (Americas 42%, EMEA 32%); 44% of APAC buyers and bidders were millennials or younger (Christie’s 2024 results)","note":"The trough. Christie’s total fell 6% to $5.7bn, auctions down 16% to $4.2bn, while private sales rose 41% to $1.5bn (27% of the total). Only one lot passed $100m worldwide: Mica Ertegun’s estate Magritte ($121.2m, 10 minutes of bidding, won by an anonymous collector through Alex Rotter who ‘needed currency conversion’), inside a Christie’s-guaranteed collection that made $194m across four sales. Sotheby’s sold Sydell Miller’s Monet ‘Nymphéas’ for $65.5m. At Sotheby’s May Modern sale about two-thirds of the value was pre-sold through house guarantees laid off to irrevocable bidders; Eduardo Costantini bought the Carrington in the room and Jen Hua’s phone took the Monet ‘Meules’. The Apex stegosaurus ($44.6m) went to Kenneth Griffin.","src":[["Artdependence, Christie’s 2024 results","https://artdependence.com/articles/christies-projects-57b-global-sales-in-2024-down-from-last-year-s-62-b"],["Apollo, ‘Handle with care’","https://apollo-magazine.com/?p=1031310"],["Artnet News, November 2024","https://news.artnet.com/market/christies-magritte-ruscha-sale-2572349"],["Artnet News, top lots November 2024","https://news.artnet.com/market/top-auction-lots-november-2024-2590194"],["Artnet News, Sotheby’s Modern evening sale, May 2024","https://news.artnet.com/market/sothebys-modern-evening-sale-2024-2487846"]],"vf":"S"},{"y":2025,"guaranteed_share":"ArtTactic, H1 2025: a record ~73% of hammer value in post-war and contemporary evening sales at the three houses was guaranteed ($392m), and 72% in Impressionist and modern (Apollo); November 2025: 59 of 80 lots (74%) in Christie’s 20th Century double-header were guaranteed (The Art Newspaper); about 22 of 66 lots (a third) in Sotheby’s 20 November sales","asia_share":"Christie’s: Asia-Pacific buyers 23% of auction sales in 2025 (down from 26%), APAC spend down 3%, Hong Kong the top APAC buyer market; APAC supplied 30% of new-buyer spend and 40% of millennial/Gen Z spend (The Value, Christie’s 2025 numbers)","note":"The recovery, driven by estates and guarantees. Art Basel/UBS put global sales at $59.6bn (+4%) and public auctions at $20.7bn (+9%), with the value of lots over $10m up 30% and all ten top lots sold in New York. Sotheby’s projected $7bn (+17%), auctions $5.7bn (+26%); Christie’s $6.2bn (+6%), auctions $4.7bn, private sales $1.5bn (24%). The Leonard Lauder estate’s 24 lots made $527.4m with fees, led by the Klimt Lederer portrait ($205m hammer after nearly 20 minutes, to Julian Dawes’s phone, over four phones and one woman in the room); the Weis collection ($218m) was all but three lots third-party guaranteed, and the two house-backed lots that failed ‘became property of Christie’s’; the Pritzkers’ Van Gogh went to Patti Wong in the room ($54m hammer); Basquiat ‘Crowns’ went to an Asian collector through Jen Hua. Sotheby’s Asia chairman Evelyn Lin called Asian participation ‘a defining force’. Other $40m+ lots found but not added: Monet ‘Nymphéas’ ($45.4m) and Hockney ‘Christopher Isherwood and Don Bachardy’ ($44.3m), both Christie’s New York 17 November 2025; the Riggio Mondrian and the Canaletto are in the lot list.","src":[["Art Basel, Global Art Market Report 2026","https://www.artbasel.com/stories/the-art-basel-and-ubs-global-art-market-report-2026"],["The Art Newspaper, 17 December 2025","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2025/12/17/christies-and-sothebys-end-2025-with-increased-sales-thanks-to-luxury-goods-trophy-lots-and-private-deals"],["The Value, Christie’s 2025 in numbers","https://en.thevalue.com/articles/christies-auction-house-global-sales-six-point-two-billion-us-dollars-2025"],["Apollo, ‘Handle with care’","https://apollo-magazine.com/?p=1031310"],["The Art Newspaper, 18 November 2025","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2025/11/18/kicking-off-new-york-november-sales-christies-nets-healthy-5747m-from-double-header-20th-century-auction"],["The Art Newspaper, 21 November 2025","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2025/11/21/frida-kahlo-record-van-gogh-still-life-sothebys-auction-new-york"],["The Art Newspaper, 18 November 2025","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2025/11/18/klimt-record-sothebys-new-york-lauder-collection"],["Sotheby’s statement via Artdaily, November 2025","https://artdaily.com/news/187608/images/close.gif"]],"vf":"S"},{"y":2026,"guaranteed_share":"May 2026 New York: ‘more than half of the lots in the spring evening sales carried third-party guarantees’, about $1.4bn pre-sold (Art & Object); the Newhouse sale was ‘entirely backed by third-party guarantees’ and 30 of 47 various-owner lots were guaranteed (The Art Newspaper); all 11 Mnuchin lots guaranteed; October London: all 41 Monsoon lots guaranteed (11 by Christie’s, 30 by third parties)","asia_share":"no full-year figure yet (as of 9 November 2026); Sotheby’s said the Lewis Modigliani went to ‘an Asian collector’, and Jen Hua’s phone took the €50m Monet in Paris","note":"The boom returns on the back of four estates. New York’s spring week made about $2.5bn, more than double May 2025 (Christie’s $1.5bn, Sotheby’s $908.6m, Phillips $146m). The Newhouse estate’s 16 lots made $630.8m, fully third-party guaranteed: Pollock ‘Number 7A’ $157m hammer after two phones duelled from $107m (Newhouse paid $32m in 2000); Brancusi $93m hammer; the Agnes Gund estate’s Rothko set a record at $85m hammer; Robert Mnuchin’s estate Rothko made $74m hammer in a fully guaranteed 11-lot sale. In June Sotheby’s London sold the Lewis Collection for £306.6m (Sotheby’s figure), a European single-owner record, and a Monet ‘Nymphéas’ hammered at £40.8m to one of five bidders. In October Peter Simon’s Monsoon collection (£107.5m) and the restituted Lewenstein Kandinsky (£27m hammer, third-party guaranteed, to Maria Los’s Americas phone) led London, and the Schlumberger Monet ‘Iris’ took €50m in Paris after 45 minutes and six bidders. Other $40m+ lots found in May 2026 but not added: Lichtenstein ‘Anxious Girl’ ($46m), Twombly ‘Untitled’ 1961 ($45.4m), Picasso ‘Homme à la guitare’ ($40.8m), all Christie’s New York 18 May 2026.","src":[["Art & Object, June 2026","https://cdn.artandobject.com/news/closer-look-25-billion-spring-auction-rebound"],["The Art Newspaper, 19 May 2026","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2026/05/19/christies-nets-11bn-from-back-to-back-si-newhouse-and-20th-century-evening-sales-in-new-york"],["The Art Newspaper, 15 May 2026","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2026/05/15/robert-mnuchins-857m-rothko-leads-sothebys-4075m-auction-in-new-york"],["Sotheby’s, London summer season report, June 2026","https://www.sothebys.com/en/articles/editorial-the-london-sales-summer-season-achieve-the-highest-total-ever-in-europe"],["The Art Newspaper, 15 October 2026","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2026/10/15/christies-achieves-£219m-in-steady-solid-london-sales"],["The Art Newspaper, 23 October 2026","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2026/10/23/monets-monumental-iris-makes-56m-in-paris"]],"vf":"S"}];
const NET_NEW_LOTS=[{"id":"picasso-femme-assise-pr-s-dune-fen-tre-marie-th-r-se","artist":"Pablo Picasso","title":"Femme assise près d’une fenêtre (Marie-Thérèse)","house":"christies","city":"New York","date":"2021-05-13","price":103.4,"cat":"Impressionist & Modern","year":"","rec":[],"note":"Not reported; Christie’s own results page could not be opened (anti-bot challenge)."},{"id":"warhol-white-disaster-white-car-crash-19-times","artist":"Andy Warhol","title":"White Disaster (White Car Crash 19 Times)","house":"sothebys","city":"New York","date":"2022-11-16","price":84.5,"cat":"Post-War & Contemporary","year":"","rec":[],"note":"Not reported."},{"id":"rothko-no-7","artist":"Mark Rothko","title":"No. 7","house":"sothebys","city":"New York","date":"2021-11-15","price":82.5,"cat":"Post-War & Contemporary","year":"","rec":[],"note":"Court-ordered divorce sale; proceeds split between the Macklowes under the judgment. Macklowe I: $588.7m hammer / $676.1m with fees."},{"id":"magritte-lempire-des-lumi-res-1961","artist":"René Magritte","title":"L’empire des lumières (1961)","house":"sothebys","city":"London","date":"2022-03-02","price":79.8,"cat":"Impressionist & Modern","year":"","rec":[],"note":"A patron’s family selling a direct-from-the-artist picture after 61 years."},{"id":"monet-le-parlement-soleil-couchant","artist":"Claude Monet","title":"Le Parlement, soleil couchant","house":"christies","city":"New York","date":"2022-05-12","price":75.96,"cat":"Impressionist & Modern","year":"","rec":[],"note":"An estate sale: the Bass sale made $363.1m, 129% above its low estimate. Christie’s does not say where the proceeds go."},{"id":"monet-le-bassin-aux-nymph-as","artist":"Claude Monet","title":"Le Bassin aux nymphéas","house":"sothebys","city":"New York","date":"2021-05-12","price":70.35,"cat":"Impressionist & Modern","year":"","rec":[],"note":"Not reported."},{"id":"rothko-untitled-shades-of-red","artist":"Mark Rothko","title":"Untitled (Shades of Red)","house":"christies","city":"New York","date":"2022-05-12","price":66.8,"cat":"Post-War & Contemporary","year":"","rec":[],"note":"Estate sale; see Monet ‘Le Parlement’."},{"id":"monet-waterloo-bridge-soleil-voil","artist":"Claude Monet","title":"Waterloo Bridge, soleil voilé","house":"christies","city":"New York","date":"2022-11-09","price":64.5,"cat":"Impressionist & Modern","year":"","rec":[],"note":"Net proceeds to the Paul G. Allen Family Foundation."},{"id":"pollock-number-17","artist":"Jackson Pollock","title":"Number 17","house":"sothebys","city":"New York","date":"2021-11-15","price":61.2,"cat":"Post-War & Contemporary","year":"","rec":[],"note":"Court-ordered divorce sale; proceeds split between the Macklowes."},{"id":"twombly-untitled-2007","artist":"Cy Twombly","title":"Untitled (2007)","house":"sothebys","city":"New York","date":"2021-11-15","price":58.9,"cat":"Post-War & Contemporary","year":"","rec":[],"note":"Court-ordered divorce sale; proceeds split between the Macklowes."},{"id":"monet-nymph-as-1907","artist":"Claude Monet","title":"Nymphéas (1907)","house":"christies","city":"New York","date":"2022-05-12","price":56.5,"cat":"Impressionist & Modern","year":"","rec":[],"note":"Estate sale; see Monet ‘Le Parlement’."},{"id":"johns-small-false-start","artist":"Jasper Johns","title":"Small False Start","house":"christies","city":"New York","date":"2022-11-09","price":55.3,"cat":"Post-War & Contemporary","year":"","rec":[],"note":"Net proceeds to the Paul G. Allen Family Foundation."},{"id":"pollock-number-31-1949","artist":"Jackson Pollock","title":"Number 31 (1949)","house":"christies","city":"New York","date":"2022-05-12","price":54.2,"cat":"Post-War & Contemporary","year":"","rec":[],"note":"Not reported."},{"id":"basquiat-museum-security-broadway-meltdown","artist":"Jean-Michel Basquiat","title":"Museum Security (Broadway Meltdown)","house":"sothebys","city":"New York","date":"2026-05-14","price":52.7,"cat":"Post-War & Contemporary","year":"","rec":[],"note":"A collector realising roughly 3.6 times a 2013 price; Sotheby’s contemporary evening sale $223m hammer / $266.8m with fees."},{"id":"manet-le-grand-canal-venise","artist":"Édouard Manet","title":"Le Grand Canal à Venise","house":"christies","city":"New York","date":"2022-11-09","price":51.9,"cat":"Impressionist & Modern","year":"","rec":[],"note":"Net proceeds to the Paul G. Allen Family Foundation."}];
const TRANSFORM={"asof":"2026-11-09","stages":[{"id":"cash","n":"From cash to a bank balance","rule":"United States: a bank files a Currency Transaction Report for cash over $10,000 in a business day; any trade or business, an auction house or dealer included, files Form 8300 for cash over $10,000 in one transaction or related transactions, and may file one voluntarily for a suspicious smaller payment; cash or monetary instruments over $10,000 carried across the border go on a CMIR; foreign accounts over $10,000 in aggregate go on the FBAR (all per FinCEN’s 2022 alert). Treasury’s 2022 study found most market institutions ‘will not accept cash in excess of $10,000 on an annual basis from a single customer’. United Kingdom: a trader that makes or receives cash payments of £10,000 or more in total is a High Value Dealer (physical cash only, not transfers) and must register with HMRC; since 14 May 2025 HVDs and art market participants must report to OFSI as soon as practicable any known or suspected designated person or breach, on pain of up to six months’ imprisonment and OFSI penalties of up to £1m or 50% of the funds. European Union: Regulation (EU) 2024/1624 sets a uniform cap of EUR 10,000 on cash payments in the commercial trade of goods from 10 Jul 2027 (member states may keep lower caps, Greece EUR 500); dealers and brokers in cultural goods of EUR 10,000 or more, and traders and warehouse keepers in free zones and bonded warehouses for such goods, stay obliged entities. Canada: the bank files an EFT report with FINTRAC for international transfers of C$10,000 or more within 5 business days (large cash reports at C$10,000, from memory, U). Switzerland’s CHF 15,000 figure for casual customers was not opened (U).","cost":"No fee. The cost is disclosure: the report names the depositor and, for Form 8300, the person on whose behalf the cash is paid.","time":"CTR within 15 days (from memory, U); FINTRAC EFT report within 5 business days; OFSI report ‘as soon as practicable’.","flags":["Cash transactions, especially large bundles of cash; large-denomination notes such as the EUR 500 note (FATF 2023, Annex A).","Sanctioned persons paying large amounts of cash, especially in currencies not typically used in the art market (FinCEN FIN-2022-Alert002).","Buyers or sellers splitting a price into smaller payments, particularly negotiating it below £10,000; requests to pay in a mix of cash and cryptoassets (OFSI 2025).","Card payments just above EUR 10,000 and cash deposits with EUR 500 notes at a dealer with unclear provenance (FATF 2023, Box 28).","Simulated gallery sales, half by transfer and half in cash, to a politically exposed buyer (FATF 2023, Box 22, Brazil); false invoices for art already sold, used to legitimise cash deposits (Box 25)."],"src":[["FinCEN alert FIN-2022-Alert002, 16 Mar 2022","https://www.fincen.gov/sites/default/files/2022-03/FinCEN Alert Russian Elites High Value Assets_508 FINAL.pdf"],["US Treasury, Study of the Facilitation of Money Laundering and Terror Finance Through the Trade in Works of Art, Feb 2022","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/Treasury_Study_WoA.pdf"],["OFSI, Art Market Participants and High Value Dealers Threat Assessment, Jun 2025","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/68529606679778c74ec15e35/OFSI_Art_Market_Participants_and_High_Value_Dealers_Threat_Assessment.pdf"],["KPMG Germany, Changes ahead: money laundering prevention in the goods trade (EU AML Regulation), Jul 2024","https://kpmg.com/de/en/home/insights/2024/07/changes-ahead-money-laundering-prevention-in-the-goods-trade.html"],["FATF, ML/TF in the Art and Antiquities Market, Feb 2023 (full text, Norwegian government mirror)","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"],["FINTRAC, Reporting electronic funds transfers to FINTRAC (2024)","https://fintrac-canafe.canada.ca/guidance-directives/transaction-operation/eft-dt/eft-dt-eng"]],"vf":"V"},{"id":"wire","n":"From the bank to the sale room","rule":"The bank, not the house, is the regulated gate in the United States: it screens the wire against OFAC’s SDN list and files a SAR when it knows, suspects or has reason to suspect that a transaction involves illicit funds, is designed to evade the BSA, lacks a lawful purpose or facilitates sanctions evasion, with no dollar threshold and the key term FIN-2022-RUSSIALUXURY; it keeps the SAR five years and may share under s.314(b). Treasury (2022) found the houses’ AML programmes ‘purely voluntary’ and that art dealers are not BSA financial institutions; the market’s own practice (government ID, proof of address, beneficial-owner declaration, source-of-funds papers above internal thresholds, refusal of third-party payments) is carried from the terminal’s earlier research, not opened on a house page this session (U). In the UK an art market participant trading or intermediating in sales of £10,000 or more, or storing art worth £10,000 or more for a person, is registered with HMRC under the Money Laundering Regulations and reports to OFSI and, separately, to the NCA by SAR. In the EU the same EUR 10,000 duty applies (FATF paras 17–18) and from 10 Jul 2027 under Regulation 2024/1624. Dollar wires clear through New York correspondent banks and so fall under OFAC wherever the parties sit (from memory, U).","cost":"Bank wire fees only; the house’s costs come at the buy stage.","time":"SWIFT wires same day to two days (from memory, U); the house’s KYC can hold a lot’s release until the papers are complete.","flags":["Shell companies, trusts or third-party intermediaries (dealers, brokers, advisers, interior designers) buying, holding or selling art for a client, especially with a sanctioned-person link (FinCEN 2022; FATF 2023).","A wire for an all-cash purchase requested from a non-US bank where the funds come from another party’s account (FinCEN 2022).","Transactions structured through multiple intermediaries that obscure the ultimate beneficiary; complex structures with family trusts in intermediary jurisdictions; family members, ex-spouses and associates acting as proxies (OFSI 2025).","A common set of banks, individuals or addresses used for luxury-goods transactions with a sanctioned-person nexus; law firms in offshore centres specialising in such clients (FinCEN 2022).","Refusal to provide KYC information; unclear source of funds or ownership history; counterparties linked to sanctioned jurisdictions (OFSI 2025)."],"src":[["FinCEN alert FIN-2022-Alert002, 16 Mar 2022","https://www.fincen.gov/sites/default/files/2022-03/FinCEN Alert Russian Elites High Value Assets_508 FINAL.pdf"],["US Treasury study, Feb 2022","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/Treasury_Study_WoA.pdf"],["OFSI Threat Assessment, Jun 2025","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/68529606679778c74ec15e35/OFSI_Art_Market_Participants_and_High_Value_Dealers_Threat_Assessment.pdf"],["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"]],"vf":"V"},{"id":"buy","n":"From money to a work","rule":"Buyer’s premium on the hammer, spring 2026 (New York schedules): Christie’s, from Sept 2025, 27% to $1.5m, 22% from $1.5m to $8m, 15% above $8m; Sotheby’s, from Feb 2026, 28% to $2m, 22% to $8m, 15% above (the low band rose from 27% and its ceiling from $1m); Phillips, from autumn 2025, 29% to $2m, 22% to $8m, 15% above, less 1–4% for a binding written bid at or above the low estimate lodged 48 hours ahead. Sales or use tax, VAT or import VAT and duty are added where the work is delivered (see the terminal’s law files); the artist’s resale right applies on resales in the UK and EU (U, not opened). Treasury (2022): under 20% of dealer sales and about 10% of auction lots exceed $50,000, but those lots are over 85% of auction value, the segment ‘of greatest concern’.","cost":"On a $1m hammer in New York in 2026: $270,000 (Christie’s) to $290,000 (Phillips) of premium before tax, i.e. 27–29% of the hammer; 22% on the slice between $1.5m or $2m and $8m; 15% above $8m.","time":"Payment is due within a short period after the sale and the lot is released only on cleared funds and completed KYC (house practice, from memory, U).","flags":["Buyers indifferent to paying well above the work’s notional value; purchases far exceeding expected value; sellers not concerned with recovering their investment; unusually high margins (FATF 2023, Annex A; FinCEN 2022).","Clients uninterested in an object’s provenance, history, style or artist; purchases by intermediaries for an unnamed principal; PEPs and their associates (FATF 2023).","A price far from market value; a sale the counterparty wants completed within days with vague answers on origin (OFSI 2025, case study 2).","Newly ‘discovered’ works attributed to prominent artists; unique archaeological pieces said to relate to existing collections but never studied (FATF 2023)."],"src":[["Grossman LLP, Spring 2026 auction season: recent changes in buyer’s premiums, 14 May 2026","https://grossmanllp.com/strongspring-2026-auction-season-examining-recent-changes-in-buyers-premiumsstrong"],["US Treasury study, Feb 2022","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/Treasury_Study_WoA.pdf"],["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"]],"vf":"S","note":"Christie’s own fee pages refused the fetch twice (bot check); the schedules rest on a law firm’s May 2026 summary. The Art Newspaper of 17 Feb 2026 on Sotheby’s change not opened."},{"id":"hold","n":"In storage, in bond","rule":"Switzerland: since the 2016 revision of the customs rules, free ports and open customs warehouses must keep inventories to a minimum standard showing the owner’s name and address, keep an up-to-date list of tenants and sub-tenants, hold time-limited licences (five years for open customs warehouses, ten for free ports) and move a minimum of goods a year (200 and 5,000); goods declared for export may stay six months, extendable by customs on proper grounds; an exporter must say the goods are bound for a free port and name the buyer, who must have a registered office or home abroad. The Federal Audit Office found 30 of 109 checks in early 2018 wanting and counted 7 free ports and 194 open customs warehouses in 2018 (10 and 245 in 2014). FATF (2023, para. 41): Switzerland requires free ports to inventory goods and record owners’ names and domicile addresses, which customs examine; Singapore places customer due diligence and record-keeping on licensed storage facilities. EU: from 10 Jul 2027 warehouse keepers of cultural goods of EUR 10,000 or more in free zones and bonded warehouses are obliged entities. UK: an art market participant storing art worth £10,000 or more for a person is within the OFSI reporting duty (from 14 May 2025). Luxembourg’s freeport rules, the Geneva Freeport’s own tariff and the canton’s stake were not opened (U). Storage, insurance and appraisal fees are not published on the pages opened (U).","cost":"Not established on an official or operator page this session (U).","time":"Six months for goods declared for export in a Swiss free port, extendable; no limit for goods in ordinary bonded storage (U).","flags":["Storage in free zones used for anonymous transactions or to keep property beyond law enforcement’s reach; art storage described as a ‘black box’ (FATF 2023, para. 40; Treasury 2022).","Buying, keeping or cancelling insurance to protect the value of, or to cash in on the loss or theft of, art linked to a sanctioned person, where other information suggests the art is worth little (FinCEN 2022; FATF 2023).","Art storage facilities and specialist service providers are higher-risk; designated persons’ high-value goods in the UK are ‘highly likely’ unreported (OFSI 2025).","Imported or exported items not declared to customs (FATF 2023)."],"src":[["Swiss Federal Audit Office, Follow-up of the evaluation of supervisory activities at free ports and open customs warehouses, CDF-17458, 8 Apr 2019","https://efk.admin.ch/wp-content/uploads/publikationen/berichte/wirtschaft_und_verwaltung/oeffentliche_finanzen_und_steuern/17458/17458be_wik_e.pdf"],["Artnet News, Switzerland approves stricter free-port rules from 1 Jan 2016","https://news.artnet.com/market/switzerland-freeport-regulations-367361"],["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"],["US Treasury study, Feb 2022","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/Treasury_Study_WoA.pdf"],["OFSI Threat Assessment, Jun 2025","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/68529606679778c74ec15e35/OFSI_Art_Market_Participants_and_High_Value_Dealers_Threat_Assessment.pdf"],["KPMG Germany on the EU AML Regulation, Jul 2024","https://kpmg.com/de/en/home/insights/2024/07/changes-ahead-money-laundering-prevention-in-the-goods-trade.html"]],"vf":"V"},{"id":"borrow","n":"From a work to a loan","rule":"Sotheby’s Financial Services (page of 2 Dec 2025, modified 15 Oct 2026): loans ‘from $1 million to over $250 million’, secured on the appraised value under Sotheby’s own appraisal standards, closing in about six weeks, with the art often left in the borrower’s possession subject to insurance and security conditions or else in approved storage; ‘no credit checks, income verification, or personal financial disclosures’ beyond an eligibility check; proceeds ‘can be used for nearly any purpose’; $12bn originated since founding; the Deloitte/ArtTactic Art & Finance Report 2025 is cited for a 2025 auction-house loan book of $3.5–5.0bn. Market (Masterworks, Jun 2026, citing Deloitte): $34–40bn of art-secured loans outstanding going into 2026, growing 8–12% a year from about $8bn in 2015; lenders are the private banks (JPMorgan, Bank of America, Citi, UBS, Goldman Sachs), the houses (Sotheby’s, book about $1.6bn; Christie’s from about $1m) and boutiques (Athena from about $2m; The Fine Art Group $1m–200m); loan-to-value capped at around 50%, typically 30–50% and 40% or less in thin markets, with haircuts to peak prices; quoted rates from 2–3% to 9% or more. Treasury (2022): lenders ‘do not have a legal obligation to collect any information on the ultimate owner of the art or the source of funds’; a bank lender is itself a BSA institution and applies its own KYC (U). The UCC-1 filing, SOFR-based pricing, term lengths and the rule that proceeds go to the borrower’s own account were not found on a page opened this session (U).","cost":"Interest at 2–3% to 9%+ (secondary, 2026); appraisal, insurance and storage at the borrower’s charge (U).","time":"About six weeks to close (Sotheby’s Financial Services).","flags":["A borrower unwilling to provide identification, who repays early or repays in cash (FATF 2023, Annex A).","A loan against a forged or inflated appraisal: forged paintings valued at over EUR 200m used as collateral in Italy, detected by an expert (FATF 2023, Box 30).","Art finance used to layer transactions and hide the initial illicit source of funds (Treasury 2022).","Loan proceeds wired to a third party or to a jurisdiction unconnected to the borrower (compliance practice; not on a page opened this session, U)."],"src":[["Sotheby’s Financial Services, Art-Backed Lending 101, 2 Dec 2025 (modified 15 Oct 2026)","https://www.sothebys.com/en/articles/art-backed-lending-101-unlocking-the-value-of-your-fine-art-collection"],["Masterworks Research, Art lending market size and growth: where the industry stands in 2026, 10 Jun 2026","https://www.masterworks.com/academy/posts/art-lending-market-size-and-growth-where-the-industry-stands-in-2026"],["US Treasury study, Feb 2022","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/Treasury_Study_WoA.pdf"],["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"]],"vf":"S","note":"Deloitte’s report itself was not opened; its figures arrive through Sotheby’s and Masterworks."},{"id":"sell","n":"From a work back to money","rule":"The seller pays a commission ‘agreed at the time of consignment’ that varies by category, value and expected demand (Christie’s help page; no figure published). Taxes on the gain: United States 28% maximum federal rate on long-term gains on collectibles plus the 3.8% net investment income tax; United Kingdom CGT on chattels over £6,000; France a flat tax on sales of works of art (6.5% including CRDS) or, on election, the ordinary regime; Switzerland no tax on private capital gains; Canada 50% inclusion with the C$1,000 listed-personal-property floor. These rates are carried from memory and were not opened this session (U). The artist’s resale right is deducted on EU and UK resales (U). The proceeds go to the consignor’s own account (house practice, U).","cost":"Seller’s commission (unpublished, negotiated) plus the tax on the gain.","time":"Settlement typically 35 days after the sale, once the buyer has paid (house practice, from memory, U).","flags":["A seller not concerned with recovering the original investment; a sale far above or below expected value (FATF 2023; FinCEN 2022).","Sudden transfers, including sales, of high-value assets by sanctioned persons (FinCEN 2022).","Proceeds to be paid to someone other than the consignor, or to a different country (compliance practice, U)."],"src":[["Christie’s, Understanding auction fees (help library)","https://www.christies.com.cn/en/help/auction-help-library/understanding-auction-fees"],["FinCEN alert FIN-2022-Alert002","https://www.fincen.gov/sites/default/files/2022-03/FinCEN Alert Russian Elites High Value Assets_508 FINAL.pdf"]],"vf":"U","note":"Tax rates pending; no tax authority page opened yet for this stage."},{"id":"give","n":"From a work to a tax deduction","rule":"United States (IRS Art Appraisal Services page, updated 28 Jun 2026): a qualified appraisal is required where the claimed deduction for an item or group of similar items is more than $5,000, with Form 8283 Section B attached to the return; a Statement of Value may be requested for an item appraised at $50,000 or more, for a user fee of $8,400 for one to three items and $800 for each further item; claimed values are reviewed by Art Appraisal Services and the Commissioner’s Art Advisory Panel (up to 25 unpaid experts, since 1968), which generally sees works valued above $150,000. The related-use rule for tangible personal property and the overvaluation penalties are not on that page (U). United Kingdom (DCMS guidance): the Cultural Gifts Scheme gives individuals a tax reduction of 30% of the agreed value against income tax or CGT, spread over up to five years, and companies 20% against corporation tax, for objects the Minister finds ‘pre-eminent for its national, scientific, historic or artistic interest’, assessed by the Acceptance in Lieu Panel and administered by the Arts Council; the CGS and Acceptance in Lieu (works accepted in settlement of inheritance tax) share a £40m annual limit; the recipient may not dispose of the object without the Minister’s consent. France’s dation en paiement was not opened (U).","cost":"The appraisal and, if sought, the IRS Statement of Value fee ($8,400 for up to three items).","time":"The IRS reviews on audit; the Acceptance in Lieu Panel recommends and the Minister decides (no time stated).","flags":["Donated art valued far above the price paid or above market; claims of a deduction based on an inflated appraisal (compliance practice; FATF 2023 lists overpricing generally).","Insurance taken or cancelled on donated high-value art linked to a sanctioned person (FinCEN 2022).","A dealer who sold more than 100% of the ownership of works to several parties (FATF 2023, Box 12) — a warning for fractional and gift structures."],"src":[["IRS, Art Appraisal Services (page updated 28 Jun 2026)","https://irs.gov/appeals/art-appraisal-services"],["DCMS, Cultural Gifts Scheme guidance (scheme of 6 Dec 2011; page dated 27 Jan 2015)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a808bb540f0b6230269422b/Cultural_Gifts_Scheme_DCMS_guidance.pdf"],["FinCEN alert FIN-2022-Alert002","https://www.fincen.gov/sites/default/files/2022-03/FinCEN Alert Russian Elites High Value Assets_508 FINAL.pdf"],["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"]],"vf":"V"},{"id":"exit","n":"From the proceeds to another country","rule":"Canada: the receiving or sending bank files an EFT report with FINTRAC for any international transfer of C$10,000 or more, or two or more in 24 hours known to share a party, within 5 business days, naming requester, beneficiary, third parties, account holders and how the funds were obtained; no permission is needed, the control is the record. United States: no exchange control; CMIR at $10,000 for cash, FBAR for foreign accounts (FinCEN 2022). United Kingdom and EU: no exchange control; the banks’ AML duties and, for designated persons, the asset freeze apply (OFSI 2025). Bangladesh: a resident may not pay a non-resident or buy foreign exchange for a capital transaction without Bangladesh Bank’s permission (FERA 1947 ss.4–5); see law_more.json for the ten capital-control entries.","cost":"Bank fees; the cost is the report.","time":"5 business days for the FINTRAC report.","flags":["Art collector’s cross-border transfers among France, Luxembourg and Switzerland (FATF 2023, Box 27); gallery manager receiving EUR 7m through a foreign trust, only EUR 2m declared to customs (Box 24).","Dealer proceeds hidden in a foreign account (FATF 2023, Box 29).","Transfers inconsistent with the customer’s profile or to a jurisdiction with no link to the customer (bank practice; not on a page opened this session, U)."],"src":[["FINTRAC, Reporting electronic funds transfers to FINTRAC (2024)","https://fintrac-canafe.canada.ca/guidance-directives/transaction-operation/eft-dt/eft-dt-eng"],["FinCEN alert FIN-2022-Alert002","https://www.fincen.gov/sites/default/files/2022-03/FinCEN Alert Russian Elites High Value Assets_508 FINAL.pdf"],["Foreign Exchange Regulation Act 1947 (Bangladesh)","https://cdn2.ogrlegal.com/files/bida/act/Foreign-Exchange-Regulation-Act-1947.pdf"],["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"]],"vf":"V"}],"typologies":[{"n":"Over- or under-invoicing between related parties: a work imported on an invoice of $100 though appraised at about $8m (Banco Santos case, Treasury 2022); contemporary paintings sold at about $30,000 each with reported prices of no more than $2,000 (FATF 2023, Box 10).","catch":"Customs valuation and the appraisal on file; the FATF indicator ‘declared value far below estimated value’; the US Form 8300 and the customs entry naming the importer; Switzerland’s inventory with the owner’s name.","src":[["US Treasury study, Feb 2022","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/Treasury_Study_WoA.pdf"],["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"]],"vf":"V"},{"n":"A third party pays or an intermediary buys for an undisclosed principal: advisers and interior designers buying with their own funds for a client with no duty to disclose the end client (Treasury 2022); the Rotenberg shell companies buying through agents after their 2014 designation (Treasury 2022, citing the Senate PSI report).","catch":"The house’s beneficial-owner declaration and refusal of third-party payment; the bank’s SAR and OFAC screening of the payer; the FinCEN red flag on intermediaries linked to sanctioned persons; the UK OFSI reporting duty on art market participants.","src":[["US Treasury study, Feb 2022","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/Treasury_Study_WoA.pdf"],["FinCEN alert FIN-2022-Alert002","https://www.fincen.gov/sites/default/files/2022-03/FinCEN Alert Russian Elites High Value Assets_508 FINAL.pdf"]],"vf":"V"},{"n":"A loan against an inflated or forged appraisal: forged paintings valued at over EUR 200m pledged as collateral in Italy (FATF 2023, Box 30); lenders with no legal duty to identify the ultimate owner or the source of funds (Treasury 2022).","catch":"The lender’s independent appraisal and expert examination (the Italian case was detected by an expert); the bank lender’s own KYC; the FATF indicator on borrowers who refuse ID or repay early or in cash.","src":[["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"],["US Treasury study, Feb 2022","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/Treasury_Study_WoA.pdf"]],"vf":"V"},{"n":"A shell-company owner in a freeport: more than 1,600 works traded through shell companies and trusts (ICIJ, cited by FATF 2023, para. 65); storage facilities as ‘black boxes’ where items sit anonymously and indefinitely (Treasury 2022).","catch":"Switzerland’s 2016 inventory rule (owner’s name and address, tenant list, six-month limit for export goods, customs inspection); Singapore’s CDD duty on licensed storage; the EU’s 2027 obliged-entity status for free-zone warehouse keepers of cultural goods of EUR 10,000 or more; beneficial-ownership registers.","src":[["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"],["Swiss Federal Audit Office, CDF-17458, 8 Apr 2019","https://efk.admin.ch/wp-content/uploads/publikationen/berichte/wirtschaft_und_verwaltung/oeffentliche_finanzen_und_steuern/17458/17458be_wik_e.pdf"],["KPMG Germany on the EU AML Regulation, Jul 2024","https://kpmg.com/de/en/home/insights/2024/07/changes-ahead-money-laundering-prevention-in-the-goods-trade.html"]],"vf":"V"},{"n":"A fractional scheme: a dealer sold ‘a total of more than 100 percent ownership in an artwork’ to several buyers, pledged works as loan collateral without the co-owners’ knowledge and gave investors fraudulent contracts, including ‘a contract that listed a stolen identity as the seller’ (Inigo Philbrick, DOJ SDNY, 23 May 2022; FATF 2023, Box 12).","catch":"Title and lien searches (the UCC record of a pledge), the contract naming the true seller, the lender’s and buyer’s provenance checks; in Philbrick’s case a lender’s default notice on a $14m loan in Oct 2019 and investors’ civil suits by Nov 2019, then the FBI Art Crime Team.","src":[["US Attorney SDNY, Former art dealer sentenced to 7 years for $86 million fraud scheme, 23 May 2022","https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/former-art-dealer-sentenced-7-years-86-million-fraud-scheme"],["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"]],"vf":"V"},{"n":"A fictitious sale or an auction bid-up by a confederate: an accomplice buys a work consigned by the launderer using criminal funds and the launderer receives a clean transfer from the auctioneer (FATF 2023, para. 67); isolated house experts able to hide identities or manipulate estimates (para. 38); NFT wash-trading (Box 5).","catch":"The house’s KYC on both consignor and buyer, the Form 8300 or bank SAR on the buyer’s funds, the FATF indicators on buyers indifferent to price and sellers indifferent to recovering their investment.","src":[["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"]],"vf":"V"},{"n":"Art as a bribe: corrupt officials paid in high-value art rather than by transfer (FATF 2023, para. 25, Box 9); bribe proceeds spent on art for a Nigerian minister’s homes (Treasury 2022).","catch":"PEP screening by the house and the bank; the FATF indicator on PEPs and associates; civil forfeiture.","src":[["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"],["US Treasury study, Feb 2022","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/Treasury_Study_WoA.pdf"]],"vf":"V"}],"roundtrip":{"in":"27–29% of the hammer in premium at the New York houses in 2026 (Christie’s 27% to $1.5m from Sept 2025; Sotheby’s 28% to $2m from Feb 2026; Phillips 29% to $2m from autumn 2025; all 22% to $8m and 15% above), plus sales tax, VAT or import charges where the work lands (New York City 8.875% on hammer plus premium for a work delivered there, from the terminal’s v24 law file, not re-opened, U; nil in a bonded store or a no-tax state). Worked: a $1,000,000 hammer at Christie’s New York delivered in Manhattan costs $1,270,000 plus $112,713 tax = $1,382,713, i.e. 38.3% above the hammer; in bond, 27%.","hold":"Storage, insurance and periodic appraisal: not established on an official or operator page this session (U); the Swiss rules add no fee but a six-month limit on goods declared for export.","out":"A negotiated seller’s commission (unpublished; 10% assumed below, U) plus the tax on any gain (US 28% federal maximum on collectibles plus 3.8% NIIT, UK CGT, France’s 6.5% flat levy, Switzerland none for private persons, Canada 50% inclusion — all from memory, U) and, in the EU and UK, the artist’s resale right (U).","years":"At break-even there is no gain and so no tax: the work must resell at a hammer that, after a 10% seller’s commission, returns the all-in cost. Delivered in Manhattan: resale hammer of $1,536,348, a rise of 53.6% over the original hammer, which takes 8.8 years at 5% a year, 6.3 years at 7% and 5.3 years at 8.5%. Held in bond: resale hammer of $1,411,111, a rise of 41.1%, 7.1 years at 5%, 5.1 years at 7%, 4.2 years at 8.5%. The 8.5% is the Mei Moses ten-year art return to mid-2006 (Michael Moses, Opalesque, Jan 2007; S); the long-run Mei Moses figure, Artprice and Art Basel/UBS return series were not opened (U). Premium and tax are computed from the stated schedules; the commission and return assumptions are the terminal’s, flagged U.","src":[["Grossman LLP, Spring 2026 auction season: recent changes in buyer’s premiums, 14 May 2026","https://grossmanllp.com/strongspring-2026-auction-season-examining-recent-changes-in-buyers-premiumsstrong"],["Opalesque, Michael Moses Q&A, 15 Jan 2007","https://www.opalesque.com/asquare/70/Michael_Moses_Q_A70.html"]],"vf":"S","note":"Sotheby’s Mei Moses page refused the fetch. The arithmetic is the terminal’s own on the cited schedules."},"cases":[{"n":"1MDB (Malaysia/United States, 2009–2015)","what":"More than $4.5bn allegedly misappropriated from the Malaysian fund; part laundered through fine art (Monet, Van Gogh) bought by a shell company and ‘gifted’ to co-conspirators by gift letters.","how_caught":"US Department of Justice civil forfeiture (Kleptocracy Asset Recovery Initiative); the works were surrendered and sold.","src":[["FATF, Feb 2023 (Box 6)","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"],["US Treasury study, Feb 2022","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/Treasury_Study_WoA.pdf"]],"vf":"V"},{"n":"The Rotenberg brothers (United States, 2014–2020)","what":"After their OFAC designation in March 2014 the brothers used shell companies and an adviser to buy tens of millions of dollars of art from the major houses and in private sales (over $18m traced by the Senate).","how_caught":"Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations staff report of 29 Jul 2020, cited by Treasury (2022) and FinCEN (2022).","src":[["US Treasury study, Feb 2022","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/Treasury_Study_WoA.pdf"],["FinCEN alert FIN-2022-Alert002","https://www.fincen.gov/sites/default/files/2022-03/FinCEN Alert Russian Elites High Value Assets_508 FINAL.pdf"]],"vf":"V"},{"n":"Nazem Ahmad and a UK art market participant (United Kingdom, 2019–2025)","what":"A UK AMP dealt in art sales with Nazem Ahmad, designated for financing Hizballah, despite knowing of the links, and failed to disclose.","how_caught":"Guilty plea to disclosure failings under the Terrorism Act 2000 (OFSI 2025, case study 1); the US designation of Ahmad in 2019 and the 2023 indictment are from memory (U).","src":[["OFSI Threat Assessment, Jun 2025","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/68529606679778c74ec15e35/OFSI_Art_Market_Participants_and_High_Value_Dealers_Threat_Assessment.pdf"]],"vf":"V"},{"n":"Banco Santos / Edemar Cid Ferreira (Brazil/United States)","what":"A work imported into the United States on an invoice valuing it at $100 though appraised at about $8m; art valued at $20–30m hidden, several works missing when searched.","how_caught":"Brazilian and US proceedings; US customs invoice found false (Treasury 2022; FATF 2023, Box 3).","src":[["US Treasury study, Feb 2022","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/Treasury_Study_WoA.pdf"],["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"]],"vf":"V"},{"n":"Forged collateral (Italy)","what":"Forged paintings valued at over EUR 200m used as loan collateral.","how_caught":"An expert’s examination (FATF 2023, Box 30).","src":[["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"]],"vf":"V"},{"n":"Inigo Philbrick (United States, 2016–2022)","what":"A London and Miami dealer sold more than 100% of the ownership of works (Basquiat’s Humidity, 1982; a 2010 Christopher Wool; a 2012 Rudolf Stingel of Picasso) to several buyers, pledged works as loan collateral without co-owners’ knowledge, inflated values with fraudulent contracts and records and invented a collector; forfeiture of $86,672,790.","how_caught":"A lender declared him in default on a c.$14m loan in mid-Oct 2019; investors sued in several jurisdictions by Nov 2019; he fled to Vanuatu, was arrested there on 11 Jun 2020 by the FBI Art Crime Team, pleaded guilty and was sentenced on 23 May 2022 to 84 months and two years’ supervised release (SDNY Money Laundering and Transnational Criminal Enterprises Unit).","src":[["US Attorney SDNY, Former art dealer sentenced to 7 years for $86 million fraud scheme, 23 May 2022","https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/former-art-dealer-sentenced-7-years-86-million-fraud-scheme"]],"vf":"V"},{"n":"Corruption proceeds through art (United States)","what":"About $144m of corruption proceeds laundered through art and luxury purchases (FATF 2023, Box 17); more than 2,200 artworks worth over $15m bought with biodiesel-credit fraud proceeds (Box 20, 2013); bribe proceeds spent on art for the homes of a Nigerian oil minister (Treasury 2022).","how_caught":"Civil forfeiture complaints by the US Department of Justice.","src":[["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"],["US Treasury study, Feb 2022","https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/Treasury_Study_WoA.pdf"]],"vf":"V"},{"n":"Operation Harmakis (Spain, 2016) and the Libyan antiquities","what":"A network trading Libyan antiquities looted by ISIL-linked groups through shell companies with false invoices describing them as decorative objects.","how_caught":"Spanish police with Europol (FATF 2023, Box 15).","src":[["FATF, Feb 2023","https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/570f17301cdd4df4bb3e19a70c640e90/rapport-money-laundering-and-terrorist-financing-in-the-art-and-antiquities-market-feb-2023.pdf"]],"vf":"V"}],"dhaka":{"n":"Cash in Dhaka → a sale room → the Geneva freeport → a bank account in Toronto: where the gates are","gates":["Dhaka: cash into a bank — the BFIU cash-transaction report (BDT 1m, U) and the bank’s source-of-funds questions; then FERA 1947 ss.4–5: no purchase of foreign exchange or payment to a non-resident for a capital purpose without Bangladesh Bank’s permission, which has no published route for a private art purchase abroad (V on the Act; the absence of a route is this session’s finding, S).","The sale room (London, New York, Geneva): the house’s KYC and beneficial-owner declaration; in the UK the AMP’s HMRC registration and OFSI reporting duty (V); in the US the bank’s SAR and Form 8300 (V); third-party payments refused (U).","Geneva freeport: the inventory with the owner’s name and address, the tenant list, the six-month limit for export goods and customs inspection (V on the Federal Audit Office; Artnet S).","Toronto: the receiving bank’s FINTRAC EFT report at C$10,000 naming sender and beneficiary within 5 business days, the bank’s KYC and suspicious-transaction report; the CRA sees the same data (V on FINTRAC; CRA from memory, U). The art dealer in Toronto is not a FINTRAC reporting entity (S/U).","Export from Canada later: a permit under the Cultural Property Export and Import Act only if the work is 50 years old, by a dead artist and above the Group V thresholds (V)."],"src":[["Foreign Exchange Regulation Act 1947 (Bangladesh)","https://cdn2.ogrlegal.com/files/bida/act/Foreign-Exchange-Regulation-Act-1947.pdf"],["FINTRAC, Reporting electronic funds transfers to FINTRAC","https://fintrac-canafe.canada.ca/guidance-directives/transaction-operation/eft-dt/eft-dt-eng"],["Swiss Federal Audit Office, CDF-17458, 8 Apr 2019","https://efk.admin.ch/wp-content/uploads/publikationen/berichte/wirtschaft_und_verwaltung/oeffentliche_finanzen_und_steuern/17458/17458be_wik_e.pdf"],["OFSI Threat Assessment, Jun 2025","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/68529606679778c74ec15e35/OFSI_Art_Market_Participants_and_High_Value_Dealers_Threat_Assessment.pdf"],["Cultural Property Export and Import Act, s.4","https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/c-51/section-4.html"]],"vf":"S"}};
